+## Version2 remediation and preserved HOLD
+
+Original SOURCE1 subject173b2fa..., both actual independent HOLD reports and its
+zero-control/runtime status are preserved unchanged. The following are SOURCE
+findings, not observed runtime vulnerabilities, leaks or failed diagnostics:
+
+- SEC-E02-TRANSPORT-DIAG-DEPENDENCY-CLOSURE-001 (MEDIUM): current host and guest
+  checks now close the **complete** installed tree:816 regular files,65 directory
+  paths and exactly two existing relative in-tree .bin symlinks. Each runtime
+  descriptor maps canonically to the frozen source-relative member. Additions,
+  including nested ws/node_modules/bufferutil, omissions, changed bytes, extra or
+  escaping/dangling symlinks, special files, malformed paths and source/runtime
+  aliases refuse. No optional module is imported in DATA models. Host validation
+  precedes helper import/container activation; guest validation precedes dynamic
+  ws import; guest completion and host post-cleanup end boundary recheck current
+  membership/bytes. Readonly bind mounts do not freeze the host tree. These
+  boundaries detect drift under the existing trusted-host assumptions, not a
+  hostile-host or portable module-load attestation.
+- QA-E02-QUERY-TERMINAL-EVIDENCE-001 and
+  SEC-E02-TRANSPORT-DIAG-TERMINAL-WRITE-002 (MEDIUM): one terminal claim prevents
+  reentry. The first incoming protocol/guard/timeout fault remains primary;
+  otherwise the first required observation failure becomes primary. Subsequent
+  phase/outcome/cleanup failures remain explicit secondary retention errors.
+  Every active timer/error/close/raw callback enters that boundary. Independent
+  finally cleanup terminates the exact socket once and settles the Promise once,
+  regardless of required sink failure; no outside timeout provides settlement.
+  Any required retention failure rejects and prevents a subsequent query/result.
+  Runtime socket cleanup has an idempotent wrapper across exchange/service paths.
+- QA-E02-QUERY-PHASE-WRITE-002 (MEDIUM): the writer makes exactly one writeSync
+  call per record and requires a full integer acknowledgement. Short/zero/invalid
+  counts, throw, overflow or serialization failure permanently poison observation.
+  No repair loop/rewrite/retry or subsequent guarded send/query/success follows.
+  State preserves intended bytes, actual reported count and acknowledged prefix
+  where known. On a throwing write the acknowledgement is UNKNOWN even if the
+  fake sink model physically wrote a prefix. Physical byte count always remains
+  null until an external actual artifact read measures it; acknowledgements are
+  not labelled physical-write attestation. Independent error/outcome files may
+  preserve failure context but cannot repair or qualify the phase trace.
+
+Late callbacks have no new observation writes/actions after terminal claim. This
+is an explicit versioned observer behavior, not a protocol deadline or operand
+change. Active raw entry still records refusal context only before guard. Known
+socket/timer faults survive a simultaneous phase failure; a real guard refusal
+survives a failed refusal-context write. Guard entry failure itself authorizes
+nothing. Successful-path original guard positions and10s/3s/45s/55s/1800s limits,
+images, public frame bytes, ownership and cleanup remain unchanged.
+
